<title>SOUTH KOREA: South Korean strike status at 0800 GMT.</title>
<headline>South Korean strike status at 0800 GMT.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-01-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federation of Korean Trade Unions, boasting 1.2 million members, has threatened to join strikes on Tuesday that would also affect the nation's public sector.</p>
<p>Following are latest available figures on the strikes, as provided by the more militant Korean Confederation of Trade Unions, which has spearheaded industrial action since the ruling party on December 26 rammed through parliament a new labour law that allows companies to lay off workers:</p>
<p>(Figures in brackets are Ministry of Labour estimates of actual workers on strike against the total workforce):</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> SECTOR	 STRIKING UNIONS		   STRIKERS</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Metal	     26				    37,087</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> University	29				     5,171</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Hospital	  33				    19,405</p>
<p>Seoul National - 2,093 strikers (400/4,000)</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Clerical	  25					3,741</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Media		9				     8,337</p>
<p>Korea Broadcasting - 4,800 strikers (2,500/6,224)</p>
<p>Munwha Broadcasting - 2,200 strikers (1,800/3,289)</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Insurance	  1				     4,700</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Automobile	13 (excludes Hyundai Motor)   50,196</p>
<p>Kia Motors - 17,000 strikers (18,498/29,389)</p>
<p>Asia Motors - 5,627 strikers/ partial (5,618/9,159)</p>
<p>Ssangyong Motor - 7,835 strikers/ partial (6,492/11,454)</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Professional    19				    10,421</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Other		3					 567</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Hyundai Union    9				    75,800</p>
<p>Hyundai Motor - 34,008 strikers/ partial (27,000/41,125)</p>
<p>*Hyundai Heavy - 21,700 strikers/ partial (0/27,000)</p>
<p>Hyundai Motor Svc - 11,000 strikers/ partial (10,000/14,488) *</p>
<p>Note: Hyundai Heavy management says work back to normal</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> Chemical	  10				     1,164</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p> TOTAL	    176				   195,189</p>
<p>The Ministry of Labour puts total number of strikers at 65,813 from 57 companies.</p>
<p>NOTE - The confederation says strikers at two Hyundai firms, Hyundai Heavy Industries and Hyundai Mipo Dock Yard, are counted twice for both metal and Hyundai union sectors.</p>
